WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.287
SQL injection prevention is easier to
sustain when error handling, logging,

2
00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:07.105
tests, and review habits all support the
same boundary.

3
00:00:07.185 --> 00:00:11.206
Database errors should help developers and
operators understand that something

4
00:00:11.286 --> 00:00:15.189
failed, but user-facing responses should
not reveal sensitive query details,

5
00:00:15.269 --> 00:00:17.542
internal table names, configuration
values,

6
00:00:17.622 --> 00:00:19.661
or data that belongs only in trusted logs.

7
00:00:19.741 --> 00:00:21.139
Logs need a similar balance.

8
00:00:21.219 --> 00:00:23.608
Structured logs, request IDs, operation
names,

9
00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:26.368
and safe error categories can support
investigation.

10
00:00:26.448 --> 00:00:29.245
Logs should avoid secrets, credentials,
personal data,

11
00:00:29.325 --> 00:00:32.296
full sensitive request bodies, and
unnecessary query text.

12
00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:34.823
Database debugging can be useful in
development,

13
00:00:34.903 --> 00:00:38.690
but production logging should be
deliberate because logs are often copied to

14
00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.858
monitoring systems, tickets, alerts, and
long-term storage.

15
00:00:41.938 --> 00:00:44.793
Code review should look for query
construction patterns,

16
00:00:44.873 --> 00:00:46.738
not just for obviously risky strings.

17
00:00:46.818 --> 00:00:49.905
Reviewers should ask whether values are
passed as parameters,

18
00:00:49.985 --> 00:00:52.549
whether dynamic identifiers come from
allowlists,

19
00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:54.785
whether raw SQL entry points are
justified,

20
00:00:54.865 --> 00:00:58.535
and whether validation occurs before query
logic depends on request data.

21
00:00:58.615 --> 00:01:02.344
Reviewers should also inspect newly added
filters, sort options, reports,

22
00:01:02.424 --> 00:01:06.503
imports, and admin tools because these
features often add dynamic query behavior.

23
00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:10.253
Tests can confirm that query-building
paths preserve the intended shape.

24
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:13.246
Unit tests can exercise validation and
allowlist mapping.

25
00:01:13.326 --> 00:01:17.114
Integration tests can verify that
supported filters, sorting, pagination,

26
00:01:17.194 --> 00:01:18.708
and reports behave correctly.

27
00:01:18.788 --> 00:01:22.459
Negative tests can confirm that
unsupported choices are rejected safely.

28
00:01:22.539 --> 00:01:25.685
The goal is not to test the database
driver's parameter binding.

29
00:01:25.765 --> 00:01:29.727
The goal is to test the application's
decisions before the database call is made.

30
00:01:29.807 --> 00:01:31.672
Static analysis, dependency review,

31
00:01:31.752 --> 00:01:34.723
and framework conventions can help when
used consistently.

32
00:01:34.803 --> 00:01:37.600
Monitoring can also detect unexpected
database errors,

33
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.593
repeated rejected requests, or unusual
failure patterns.

34
00:01:40.673 --> 00:01:42.421
The same caution applies there too:

35
00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:46.463
detection should improve visibility
without turning observability into a data

36
00:01:46.543 --> 00:01:47.300
exposure path.
